<body>

<%- include("partials/header") %>

<main>
  <section class="about-section">
    <div class="about-inner">


      <h3 class="about-title order-title">ABOUT</h3>
      <hr class="order-rule">

      <p class="about-text">
        The Order of the Lion's Bell is an independent research and educational fellowship
        concerned with unusual phenomena, consciousness, history, philosophy,
        and other subjects which remain open to investigation.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        Our principal work is the collection and preservation of research material:
        books, papers, correspondence, reports, testimony, archival records,
        observations, and other documents which may be useful to present or future investigators.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        Particular attention is given to psychical research, anomalous experience,
        consciousness studies, UAP, folklore, unusual historical claims,
        intelligence material, and those places where several fields of inquiry meet.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        We make no requirement that a strange report must be believed because it is strange,
        or dismissed because it does not fit an accepted explanation.
        Our interest is first in obtaining the record, establishing where it came from,
        comparing it with what is already known, and preserving it well enough that the question
        may be taken up again.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        Members of the Order are known as <span class="accent">Heralds of the Lion</span>.
      </p>


      <div class="divider-symbol">✶✶</div>

      <br>
      <br>


      <h3 class="about-title order-title">THE WORK</h3>
      <hr class="order-rule">

      <p class="about-text">
        Much useful research has disappeared for no better reason than that nobody kept it.
        Small journals cease publication, private correspondence is scattered,
        websites disappear, witnesses die, collections are broken up,
        and an observation which once appeared unimportant may be impossible to recover twenty years later.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        For this reason preservation is one of the chief concerns of the Order.
        The Heralds Research Library gathers books, papers, periodicals, government records,
        old investigations, and related material from the several fields in which we work.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        The Experience Archive receives accounts of unusual experiences.
        These are recorded as faithfully as possible without requiring the witness
        or the Order to settle their explanation beforehand.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        Questions which deserve continued attention may be opened as Case Files.
        Claims, documents, testimony, photographs, correspondence, references,
        and other material can then be brought together without losing the distinction
        between what was reported and what can actually be established.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        Some cases close quickly. Others may remain open for years.
        There is no particular virtue in forcing an answer where the material does not provide one.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        Members also contribute observations, source material, notes, translations,
        bibliographies, field reports, and leads.
        The value of the archive grows by accumulation.
        One obscure report may mean very little by itself and considerably more
        when placed beside fifty others.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        Completed investigations and useful findings may be issued as
        <span class="accent">Bell Reports</span>.
        Research notes, correspondence, archival discoveries, reports from the field,
        questions requiring assistance, and notices from ongoing investigations
        are circulated through <em>The Bell</em>.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        In this respect our methods owe much to the older independent research societies:
        correspondence between investigators, private collections,
        small periodicals, case reports, shared experiments,
        and the continual exchange of material between people who happened to be interested
        in the same difficult question.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        The internet has made that exchange easier.
        The purpose remains much the same.
      </p>


      <div class="divider-symbol">✶✶</div>

      <br>
      <br>


      <h3 class="about-title order-title">WHY AN ORDER?</h3>
      <hr class="order-rule">

      <p class="about-text">
        The Lion's Bell is called an Order because research is only one part of its life.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        The Order is also a fellowship.
        It has a Rule, stages of formation, offices, chapters, correspondence,
        gatherings, a body of common reading, and certain traditions which members undertake to preserve.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        This form is drawn partly from the old learned fraternity
        and partly from the chivalric idea that knowledge carries obligations with it:
        to serve faithfully, to keep one's word, to exercise judgment,
        to assist one's fellows, and to take responsibility for what one puts into the world.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        Advancement in the Order is therefore not intended as a collection of titles.
        It marks participation in the work and increasing responsibility for its continuation.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        A Herald may be a scientist, historian, student, archivist,
        engineer, philosopher, writer, collector, or simply a careful observer.
        The Order has no interest in requiring everyone to think alike.
        It asks instead that members learn how to inquire, how to preserve a record,
        and how to disagree without destroying the work.
      </p>


      <div class="divider-symbol">✶✶</div>

      <br>
      <br>


      <h3 class="about-title order-title">TRADITIONS</h3>
      <hr class="order-rule">

      <p class="about-text">
        The Order is a modern organization.
        Its older history is a history of influence rather than a claim of documented institutional descent.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        We are interested in the many groups, schools, libraries, religious communities,
        philosophical circles, scientific societies, and private correspondents
        who attempted before us to preserve difficult knowledge
        and carry unanswered questions from one generation to the next.
      </p>


      <div class="divider-symbol">✶✶</div>


      <p class="about-subtitle">
        Sais and the Keeping of Records
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        Plato tells of Solon visiting the priests of Sais in Egypt
        and encountering a people whose records preserved events forgotten elsewhere.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        The story has long interested the Order less as proof of a secret lineage
        than as a very old statement of a problem we still face:
        civilizations forget.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        An archive is one answer to that problem.
      </p>


      <div class="divider-symbol">✶✶</div>


      <p class="about-subtitle">
        Pythagoras
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        The Pythagorean tradition remains another important influence.
        It joined the study of nature, number, philosophy,
        discipline, and fellowship rather than treating them as entirely separate pursuits.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        The Order retains something of this idea:
        the investigator himself is part of the work.
        Carelessness, vanity, credulity, dishonesty, and poor judgment
        can spoil a good investigation as easily as bad equipment.
      </p>


      <div class="divider-symbol">✶✶</div>


      <p class="about-subtitle">
        Hermes
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        Hermes is especially appropriate to an Order of Heralds.
        He is the messenger, traveller, interpreter,
        and the figure who crosses boundaries which normally remain apart.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        The Hermetic writings and the later traditions surrounding them
        form part of the historical and philosophical material studied by the Order.
        They are approached as texts and traditions to be examined,
        compared, interpreted, and placed in their proper setting.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        Hermes also reminds the researcher that information seldom arrives without difficulty.
        Reports are distorted.
        Stories acquire additions.
        Witnesses disagree.
        Documents are copied badly.
        Coincidence and expectation complicate interpretation.
        Sometimes fraud enters the picture.
        Sometimes something genuinely difficult remains.
      </p>


      <div class="divider-symbol">✶✶</div>


      <p class="about-subtitle">
        Chivalry
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        From the chivalric tradition the Order takes its language of service,
        fellowship, duty, courage, restraint, and the keeping of a common Rule.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        These ideas survive in our offices, heraldry, stages of formation,
        chapters, gatherings, and the responsibilities placed upon a Herald.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        We have little use for ceremony which has no corresponding duty.
        The title matters only insofar as the work behind it does.
      </p>


      <div class="divider-symbol">✶✶</div>


      <p class="about-subtitle">
        Independent Research
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        Our nearest practical ancestors are much more recent:
        the small research societies, private laboratories,
        correspondence groups, collectors, and independent journals
        of the nineteenth and twentieth centuries.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        They investigated subjects which universities often ignored,
        circulated observations among interested readers,
        printed arguments from opposing sides,
        and preserved a great quantity of material which might otherwise have disappeared.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        Their archives contain mistakes, exaggerations, dead ends,
        remarkable observations, forgotten experiments,
        and questions which remain just as unsettled now as when they were first printed.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        All of it is part of the record.
      </p>


      <div class="divider-symbol">✶✶</div>

      <br>
      <br>


      <h3 class="about-title order-title">THE MODERN ORDER</h3>
      <hr class="order-rule">

      <p class="about-text">
        The present Order of the Lion's Bell was established in the Mediterranean
        as an attempt to continue this kind of independent work under modern conditions.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        Instead of mimeographed circulars and filing cabinets alone,
        we now have searchable archives, digital correspondence,
        international catalogues, public databases,
        and the ability to place researchers separated by thousands of kilometres
        around the same case file.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        These are better tools.
        They do not necessarily make us better investigators.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        That part still depends upon patience,
        good records, useful criticism,
        and people willing to spend time on questions
        for which there may never be a satisfactory answer.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        Anyone may bring useful material to the attention of the Order.
        We are particularly interested in unpublished records,
        unusual personal accounts, forgotten investigations,
        obscure periodicals, archival material,
        and observations which may have been overlooked because nobody knew where to send them.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        The aim is modest enough:
        preserve what should not be lost,
        investigate what can be investigated,
        and leave the next researcher a better starting point.
      </p>


      <div class="divider-symbol">✶✶</div>

      <br>
      <br>


      <h3 class="about-title order-title">SYMBOLS OF THE ORDER</h3>
      <hr class="order-rule">

      <p class="about-text">
        The Order keeps a small body of symbols and traditional language.
        These belong to the fellowship and should not be mistaken for research conclusions.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        The <span class="accent">Lion</span> represents courage,
        guardianship, and responsibility.
        Its many appearances in religion, heraldry, philosophy,
        alchemy, and esoteric history have also made it an object of study in its own right.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        The <span class="accent">Bell</span> is our principal emblem.
        A bell calls a scattered company together.
        It announces something which ought to be heard,
        and it marks the passing of time.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        <em>Campana sonat omnibus.</em>
        The Bell rings for all.
      </p>

      <p class="about-text">
        A <span class="accent">Herald</span> carries the message.
        In practical terms this means carrying information faithfully:
        bringing reports into the archive,
        carrying useful findings outward,
        and leaving as little as possible to rumour when a record can be made instead.
      </p>


      <div class="divider-symbol">✶✶</div>


      <p class="about-quote">
        What is known should be recorded.<br>
        What is uncertain should remain open.<br>
        What is lost cannot be investigated.
      </p>


      <div class="divider-symbol">✶✶</div>


      <p class="about-quote">
        ὁ ἔχων ὦτα ἀκούειν ἀκουέτω<br>
        <span>"He who has ears to hear, let him hear."</span>
      </p>


    </div>
  </section>
</main>

<%- include("partials/footer") %>

</body>
</html>
